WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.190 --> 00:00:05.810
The Winograd schema is a test designed to
stretch computer language processing to its limit.

00:00:05.810 --> 00:00:10.360
If a machine can determine an intended referent
in a sentence based only on clues from context,

00:00:10.360 --> 00:00:14.889
then the machine is using reasoning to parse
the language, and, perhaps,

00:00:14.889 --> 00:00:17.290
it should be called intelligent.

00:00:17.290 --> 00:00:21.190
Humans are pretty good at working out referents
based on context. For example:

00:00:21.190 --> 00:00:25.500
"The trophy would not fit in the brown suitcase
because it was too big."

00:00:26.660 --> 00:00:28.600
What was too big?

00:00:28.600 --> 00:00:33.440
Linguists will sometimes denote referents
in text by using subscript numbers or letters.

00:00:33.440 --> 00:00:37.360
So if you want to solve that “x”, and work
out what it refers to in that sentence,

00:00:37.370 --> 00:00:40.480
then you have to know that a suitcase usually
contains things,

00:00:40.480 --> 00:00:43.069
and that a trophy usually
doesn’t contain things.

00:00:43.069 --> 00:00:45.589
And you have to know how containers work,

00:00:45.589 --> 00:00:47.740
that larger things can’t fit inside
smaller things.

00:00:47.740 --> 00:00:49.260
It seems simple to us,
because we understand

00:00:49.260 --> 00:00:53.740
not only how syntax works and whether
a sentence is grammatically valid,

00:00:53.740 --> 00:01:00.020
but also because we’ve also got experience of
suitcases and trophies and reality itself.

00:01:00.020 --> 00:01:03.260
Solving that “x” requires comprehension.

00:01:03.269 --> 00:01:08.180
Terry Winograd started this line of thinking
in 1972 by proposing sentences that required

00:01:08.180 --> 00:01:09.410
that sort of context.

00:01:09.410 --> 00:01:14.790
"The city councilmen refused the demonstrators
a permit because they 'feared violence'

00:01:14.790 --> 00:01:16.750
"or because they 'advocated revolution'."

00:01:16.750 --> 00:01:20.190
...it was the 70s, there weren’t many councilwomen.

00:01:20.190 --> 00:01:22.080
And there was a lot of revolution.

00:01:22.080 --> 00:01:26.570
Winograd argued solving this sentence
requires knowing not only who can issue permits

00:01:26.570 --> 00:01:27.920
or what a demonstrator is,

00:01:27.920 --> 00:01:31.140
but also the political interests of a council,

00:01:31.140 --> 00:01:33.410
the ways in which they’d view
political change.

00:01:33.410 --> 00:01:39.590
That referent changes based purely on the
context and the meaning of those last two words.

00:01:39.590 --> 00:01:42.810
Now there are plenty of sentences where the
“x” is ambiguous,

00:01:42.810 --> 00:01:47.860
where even humans don't have enough context
to work out which pronoun refers to which person.

00:01:47.860 --> 00:01:49.620
If you’re writing a gay romance story,

00:01:49.620 --> 00:01:52.460
and both your main characters
use the same pronouns

00:01:52.460 --> 00:01:55.120
and appear all the time in the same sentences,

00:01:55.130 --> 00:01:57.660
then it’s going to get confusing fast.

00:01:57.660 --> 00:01:59.710
In more amateur work, like slash fiction,

00:01:59.710 --> 00:02:04.560
inexperienced writers often try to clear that
up with synecdoche in place of pronouns.

00:02:04.560 --> 00:02:06.100
But that’s awkward.

00:02:06.100 --> 00:02:10.380
More experienced writers tend to use context
and careful sentence construction,

00:02:10.380 --> 00:02:12.200
and readers never notice it.

00:02:12.200 --> 00:02:15.560
But Winograd schemas are designed
to be unambiguous for humans,

00:02:15.560 --> 00:02:18.520
to the point where we wouldn’t even have
to think about it,

00:02:18.520 --> 00:02:20.940
but difficult for computers.

00:02:20.950 --> 00:02:23.970
Natural Language Processing, the ability to
"understand" language,

00:02:23.970 --> 00:02:26.280
is in huge demand right now;
it’s what powers Siri,

00:02:26.280 --> 00:02:28.310
and Alexa, and the Google Assistant.

00:02:28.310 --> 00:02:32.420
So many companies are trying to create code
that can understand human requests,

00:02:32.420 --> 00:02:35.770
and let’s be honest,
most of that code sucks right now.

00:02:35.770 --> 00:02:37.650
They often just rely on collections of sentences,

00:02:37.650 --> 00:02:42.670
each word tagged with its part of speech;
they just pick up on keywords like “call”

00:02:42.670 --> 00:02:46.470
or “how much is” and extract what they
hope are the right bits around them.

00:02:46.470 --> 00:02:49.150
That doesn’t work for a Winograd schema.

00:02:49.150 --> 00:02:53.630
A team of researchers at the computer science
department of New York University have assembled

00:02:53.630 --> 00:02:57.860
a list of 150 of them, as a test.

00:02:57.860 --> 00:03:03.250
So: “I spread the cloth on the table in order
to protect it.” To protect what?

00:03:03.250 --> 00:03:05.660
Obvious if you understand what a cloth and
a table are,

00:03:05.660 --> 00:03:08.470
and why you might spread a cloth on a table,

00:03:08.470 --> 00:03:11.130
but if those are just tagged as nouns with
no context,

00:03:11.130 --> 00:03:13.050
then that’s completely ambiguous.

00:03:13.050 --> 00:03:15.720
Hi. Future Tom here, just interrupting
to say that

00:03:15.720 --> 00:03:18.700
after this video was finished and done and
all the graphics were completed,

00:03:18.700 --> 00:03:22.080
I found out that some had published a new
version of GPT-2,

00:03:22.080 --> 00:03:25.000
which is the leading machine-learning
text-generation system,

00:03:25.000 --> 00:03:26.910
and I figured that if I didn't
address that here,

00:03:26.910 --> 00:03:28.599
a load of people in the comments would be all,

00:03:28.600 --> 00:03:29.760
"Well, what about machine learning?
What about AI?"

00:03:29.760 --> 00:03:32.400
So I got a version of GPT-2,

00:03:32.400 --> 00:03:35.030
it's set up kind of like a Dungeons &amp; Dragons
adventure system

00:03:35.030 --> 00:03:38.450
I told it to create a suitcase and a trophy

00:03:38.450 --> 00:03:41.310
and to put the suitcase in the trophy to see
what would happen.

00:03:41.310 --> 00:03:42.500
ARTIFICIAL VOICE:
"The moment you think about it,

00:03:42.500 --> 00:03:44.740
"you know what to do, so you do it with your
eyes closed:

00:03:44.740 --> 00:03:47.209
"you place your hand on top of it, then with
your other hand

00:03:47.209 --> 00:03:49.150
"you take out your gun
from inside your jacket pocket,

00:03:49.150 --> 00:03:51.060
"take aim at it, pull back one inch…"

00:03:51.060 --> 00:03:53.739
Yeah, if you think machine learning's going
to save us,

00:03:53.739 --> 00:03:54.740
uh, not yet!

00:03:54.740 --> 00:03:58.140
And of course, sometimes words can switch
parts of speech.

00:03:58.140 --> 00:04:00.770
"Main" is usually an adjective,
but in video games,

00:04:00.770 --> 00:04:03.110
it can be a verb or a noun.

00:04:03.110 --> 00:04:06.440
Humans who’ve not heard that use before
won’t understand it.

00:04:06.440 --> 00:04:10.800
The solution for now? Accept that Siri’s
not going to be great at complicated questions,

00:04:10.800 --> 00:04:13.810
and hire a team of underpaid contract workers
to manually tag data,

00:04:13.810 --> 00:04:16.820
in order to help programs "learn" patterns.

00:04:16.820 --> 00:04:18.970
But those methods will continue to fall short,

00:04:18.970 --> 00:04:22.560
because computers are missing the breadth
of knowledge that humans have access to.

00:04:22.560 --> 00:04:24.920
At least, for now.

00:04:24.920 --> 00:04:28.160
Artificial language processing
remains 10 years away,

00:04:28.160 --> 00:04:30.220
just as it has for the last few decades.

00:04:30.700 --> 00:04:34.910
One of my co-authors, Gretchen McCulloch,
has a wonderful podcast called "Lingthusiasm".

00:04:34.910 --> 00:04:36.590
You can listen to it at the link in the description.

